{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792203274,"nonce":"MmyaGfBh5Gfl5e2aj2u81sk1H+M=","title":"eval.vm.Gc finalisation is approximate (GcFinalizer)","message":"rayzor has no tracing collector, so runtime/src/gc.rs decides a finalised target is gone when no handle besides its own is counted and its handle no longer appears on the calling thread's stack. The scan starts at the caller's frame and does not read registers, so a dead copy of a handle left in a callee-saved register does not keep a target alive. A live reference held only in a register, or a reference stored in another heap object, is not seen: such a target can be reported collected while still in use. That is memory safe (a fired target is never freed; its handle keeps it alive), but the callback runs early.\n\nUser decision 2026-10-17: refcount-level semantics are acceptable for the eval API (\"nobody runs real apps on eval\"). A real collector (an Immix GC like Ash's) is the long-term option.","files":null}]}